
%9Syntax:%9

TKL %|[-server <tag>] [-mask ident|host|domain] [-force] [-dry] <nick|user@host> [<time>] <reason>

%9Parameters:%9

    -server:     Tag of the connection the TKLINE goes to; defaults to the
                 server of the active window.
    -mask:       Type of mask built from the WHOIS reply (default:
                 tk_mask_type):
                   ident   ident@host, '~ident' is turned into '*ident',
                   host    *@full.host,
                   domain  *@*.domain.tld, *@a.b.c.* for IPv4, the /64
                           network for IPv6 (*@2001:db8:1:2::/64);
                           ::ffff:a.b.c.d is treated as IPv4.
    -force:      Allows sending a mask considered too broad.
    -dry:        Preview: shows the final TKLINE line and sends nothing.

    <nick|user@host>: A nick (the script runs WHOIS and builds the mask
                 itself) or a ready user@host mask, sent literally.
    <time>:      Duration: 30s, 10m, 2h, 1d, 1w or a combination such as
                 1d12h; a bare number means minutes. Without a time
                 tk_default_time is used.
    <reason>:    The K-line reason, required.

%9Description:%9

    Sets a temporary K-line (TKLINE) on an ircd 2.11 server (IRCnet).
    A command of the tk.pl script; /TK without a subcommand works the same.

    For a nick the script sends WHOIS (the reply is hidden) and sends the
    TKLINE only after it has received the ident@host. If the nick does not
    exist or WHOIS does not answer within tk_whois_timeout_ms, nothing is
    sent. Your own nick cannot be used.

    The second word is taken as the time only when it parses as one;
    otherwise the rest of the line is the reason. A time without a reason
    is an error. The server gets the canonical form (e.g. 90 -> 1h30m),
    no longer than tk_max_time.

    Before sending, the script checks the mask the same way ircd does
    (user up to 10 characters, host up to 63, valid ip/prefix notation)
    and rejects a mask that matches you, as well as a mask that is too
    broad (e.g. *@*, *@*.pl), unless -force is given or tk_allow_broad is
    ON. Control characters are stripped from the reason and it is
    truncated to tk_reason_max or the server's TOPICLEN.

    Requires IRC operator status unless tk_require_oper is OFF; this also
    applies to -dry. With tk_confirm = ON the TKLINE waits 60 seconds for
    /TK YES. Server replies "Incorrect format", 481 and 421 are turned
    into readable messages. Sent commands are logged to
    ~/.erssi/tk_audit.jsonl (tk_audit).

    While your own host is unknown (erssi learns it after joining a
    channel), only an ident@full.host mask is accepted - any other could
    hit you.

%9Settings:%9

    tk_mask_type = ident
        Default mask type: ident, host or domain.
    tk_default_time = 30m
        K-line duration when none is given in the command.
    tk_max_time = 30d
        Longest allowed duration; 0 or empty = no limit.
    tk_whois_timeout_ms = 10000
        How long to wait for WHOIS (clamped to 2000-60000 ms).
    tk_reason_max = 0
        Maximum reason length; 0 = the server's TOPICLEN, or 160 without it.
    tk_require_oper = ON
        Without +o (/OPER) the TKL and UNTKL commands refuse to work.
    tk_allow_broad = OFF
        ON allows broad masks without -force.
    tk_confirm = OFF
        ON = every TKLINE requires confirmation with /TK YES.
    tk_audit = ON
        Log events to ~/.erssi/tk_audit.jsonl (JSON, mode 0600).

%9Examples:%9

    /TKL spammer advertising flood
    /TKL spammer 2h advertising flood
    /TKL -mask domain -dry spammer 1d proxy
    /TKL *bot@*.example.org 1w infected hosts
    /TKL -server ircnet -force *@203.0.113.* 30m attack from a subnet

